{
  "dom_id": "step-q334633",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm \\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"120px\" align=\"center\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td width=\"120px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
